text:
**Refined prompt:** Review the `UserService.update()` password-hashing diff as a pre-push correctness/security gate on this NestJS RealWorld codebase — held to LESSONS.md and CURRENT_FOCUS.md, every claim cited to file:line — and tell me whether it's safe to push, with the concrete fix.

Don't push this. It doesn't compile, and if you make it compile it converts an existing data-corruption bug into an account-takeover primitive.

## Blockers

**1. Missing `await` — the hash never reaches the column.** `argon2.hash()` returns `Promise<string>` (argon2 `^0.26.2`, `package.json:34`); it's awaited everywhere else in this repo — `user.entity.ts:30`, and `argon2.verify` at `user.service.ts:31`. As written, a Promise object is handed to `userRepository.save()`. The mysql driver either stringifies it to `[object Promise]` or rejects it as an invalid parameter; either way the column no longer holds an argon2 hash, `argon2.verify` at `user.service.ts:31` can never match, and the user is locked out permanently. That is precisely the failure LESSONS.md #88 records, reached by a new route.

**2. It fails to type-check.** `UpdateUserDto` declares no `password` field (`update-user.dto.ts:1-6`), so `dto.password` is TS2339, and `Promise<string>` → `string` on `updated.password` is a second error on the same line. `npm start` runs `node index.js` → `ts-node/register` (`index.js:1`), which type-checks by default, so the server won't boot. The DTO needs `readonly password?: string`.

**3. New: the response leaks the password hash.** `UserController.update()` returns the raw `UserEntity` to the client (`user.controller.ts:26-29`) — no envelope, no serializer, no `@Exclude` on `user.entity.ts:26`. Until now `delete toUpdate.password` (`user.service.ts:76`) guaranteed the key was absent, so the leak was latent. Your line puts it back. After you add the `await`, `PUT /user` with a password change responds with the argon2 hash in plain JSON. (Before the `await` fix it serializes as `"password":{}` — `JSON.stringify` of a Promise.)

## The half that's missing, and why it's the dangerous half

CURRENT_FOCUS.md:13-14 scopes this task as two things: hash on update **and** stop the raw body being merged into the entity. This diff does the first only, and the two interact badly.

`Object.assign(toUpdate, dto)` (`user.service.ts:79`) still persists every field the client sends, including `id` — `PUT /user` has no pipe (`user.controller.ts:26`, contrast `:31` and `:42`), and there's no global one (`main.ts:5-21`). So `{"user":{"id":2,"password":"x"}}` makes `save()` write to row 2. Before your change that wrote *plaintext* to the victim's row — destructive, but the attacker still couldn't log in, since `findOne` compares against a hash. With a working hash path it writes a **valid credential the attacker chose** onto another user's account. LESSONS.md #112 is the rule; this diff is what makes violating it exploitable.

Note the repo's own `ValidationPipe` won't save you here — `validation.pipe.ts:23` returns the original `value`, not the stripped class instance. It validates, it doesn't whitelist. Copy fields by name.

**Also subtle:** `if (dto.password)` guards the hashing, not the merge. `{"password": ""}` is falsy, so it skips the branch — but `Object.assign` on line 79 already copied `""` onto the entity, and `save()` writes an empty string over the hash. The guard looks like it handles this case; it doesn't.

## Suggested shape

```ts
async update(id: number, dto: UpdateUserDto): Promise<UserEntity> {
  const toUpdate = await this.userRepository.findOne(id);
  delete toUpdate.password;
  delete toUpdate.favorites;

  // copy allowed fields by name; the DTO strips nothing at runtime (LESSONS.md #112).
  // skip undefined so PUT stays a partial update
  for (const field of ['username', 'email', 'bio', 'image'] as const) {
    if (dto[field] !== undefined) toUpdate[field] = dto[field];
  }

  if (dto.password) {
    toUpdate.password = await argon2.hash(dto.password);
  }

  const saved = await this.userRepository.save(toUpdate);
  delete saved.password;
  return saved;
}
```

Plus `readonly password?: string;` in `UpdateUserDto`.

Two follow-ups I'd keep as separate commits rather than fold in here, since both change response shape:

- `PUT /user` returning a bare entity instead of `{user}` is LESSONS.md #57 — `buildUserRO` (`user.service.ts:116`) is right there and would fix the leak and the envelope in one move, but it changes what clients receive.
- Hashing now exists in two places (`user.entity.ts:28-31` and here). Worth collapsing to one helper so the argon2 options can't drift apart.

## Verification

I read the code; I did not compile or run it. Nothing covers this path — `src/tag/tag.controller.spec.ts` is the only spec in the repo — so before pushing, exercise it by hand: `PUT /api/user` with a new password, then `POST /api/users/login` with it, and confirm the `PUT` response body has no `password` key.
